> "The fastest yes isn't always the right yes — and a good advisor will tell you the difference."

## Michael's Memo: When "Approved" Isn't Good Enough

Not every "no" is really a no. And not every "yes" is really a yes.

This month's client came to us needing a straightforward $350,000 line of credit to smooth payroll gaps, vendor payables, and seasonal swings — the kind of working capital ask we see every week.

The file itself was strong: a 44-year-old commercial drywall and framing contractor, roughly $9.5M in trailing revenue, acquired by an experienced ownership group two years ago. On paper, a clean deal.

The first lender declined outright over a documentation technicality. The second came back "approved" — but only with a daily-payment structure we don't place for clients unless they're in real distress, and capped hard because the lender's time-in-business clock only started at the ownership change, not the company's 44-year history.

We told that lender directly: the offer wasn't competitive, and we said so in writing. Not because we wanted a fight, but because our client deserved the truth about what "approved" actually meant in that structure.

We kept working. A third lender took the time to look past the paper cap and see the real business behind it, and came back with a fair offer: $200,000, structured to actually work for a seasonal contractor's cash flow.

The lesson, whether you own the business or advise one: an approval isn't a win. The right structure is.

Here to serve,

Michael Kodinsky, Founder & CEO

## Deal Highlight: $200K Working Capital for a 44-Year Legacy Drywall and Framing Contractor

**Client profile**

- Georgia-based commercial drywall, steel framing, and acoustical ceiling contractor
- In business since 1982, acquired by current ownership two years ago
- Roughly $9.5M in trailing revenue, 14 employees
- Clean operating history, though one lender's file flagged a prior merchant cash advance default

**The challenge**

The client needed working capital for payroll gaps, vendor payables, seasonal swings, and opportunistic spend. One lender declined outright on a verification technicality. A second pre-approved the file, but only with a daily-payment structure, and calculated "time in business" from the two-year-old ownership change rather than the company's real 44-year history, capping size and pricing accordingly.

**The solution**

- Product: Revenue-Based Term Loan
- Amount: $200,000
- Term: 15 months
- Payments: Weekly ($4,194 per week)
- Use of proceeds: payroll gaps during seasonal swings, vendor payables, and opportunistic spend on new contracts

{% callout type="tip" title="The takeaway" %}
This deal closed in about a week once the right lender said yes, but it took turning down a technically approved offer to get there. For a legacy business with decades of history behind a recent ownership change, the "approved" answer isn't always the right one.
{% /callout %}

## More Than Money: Core Value of the Month, Transparency

At Serve Funding, Transparency doesn't just mean disclosing terms. It means telling a lender an offer isn't good enough, even when "approved" is sitting right there in the inbox.

It's easy to pass along whatever comes back and call it a win. It's harder, and more valuable, to tell a lender directly that a daily-payment structure isn't one we'll place unless a client is in real distress, and to keep working until the offer actually fits.

In practice, that means:

- We tell the business owner, and anyone advising them, the truth about an offer, not just the headline number.
- We won't place a structure that doesn't serve the business, even if it's the fastest "yes."
- We keep working past the first approval until the terms actually make sense.
- We keep the people already advising the business in the loop from start to finish.

Because an approval isn't the finish line. The right structure is. And that's what we're here to serve.
